{{smaller block|And the coast shall be for the remnant of the house of Judah, they shall feed ''thereupon.''
{{right|Zeph. ii. 7.}}}}

2. In consequence of that; by reason of that.

{{smaller block|Here is also frequently growing a certaine tall Plant, whose stalke being all ouer couered with a red rinde, is ''thereupon'' termed the red weed.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, II. 113.}}}}

3. Immediately after that; without delay; in sequence, but not necessarily in consequence.

{{smaller block|The Hostages are delivered up to K. Edward, who brought them into England; and ''thereupon'' King John is honourably conducted to Calais.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 125.}}}}

{{smaller block|He ''thereupon'' . . . without more ado sends him adrift.
{{right|''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 406.}}}}

'''Thereva''' (ther′e-vä̤), ''n.'' [NL. (Latreille, 1796), irreg. < Gr. θηρεύειν, hunt.] The typical genus of the ''Therevidæ'', containing medium-sized slender dark-colored flies. About 20 species are known in North America.

'''Therevidæ''' (thē-rev′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Westwood, 1840), < ''Thereva'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of predaceous flies resembling the ''Asilidæ'', but having the labium fleshy instead of horny. {{smaller|Their larvæ live in earth and decaying wood, and are either carnivorous or herbivorous. The adult flies feed mainly upon other dipters, for which they lie in wait upon leaves and bushes. About 200 species are known. They are sometimes called ''leaf-nosed flies''.}}

'''therewhile†''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-hwīl′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therwhile'', ''therwhyle''; < ''there'' + ''while''.] 1. Meanwhile; the while; presently.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|''Ther-while'' entred in thre maydenes of right grete bewte, wher-of tweyne were neces vn-to Agrauadain.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 607.}}}}

2. For that time.

{{smaller block|So have I doon in erthe, allas ''ther-whyle!''
That certes . . . he wol my gost exyle.
{{right|''Chaucer'', A. B. C., l. 54.}}}}

'''therewhiles†''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-hwīlz′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''ther-whiles;'' as ''therewhile'' + adv. gen. ''-es.''] During the time; while.

{{smaller block|''Therwhiles'' that thilke thinges ben idoon, they ne myhte nat ben undoon.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, v. prose 6.}}}}

'''therewith''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-wiᴛ̵ʜ′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therwith'' (= Sw. ''dervid'' = Dan. ''derved''); as ''there'' + ''with.''] 1. With that.

{{smaller block|He ȝaue ȝow fyue wittes
For to worshepen hym ''ther-with.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), ii. 16.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have learned, in whatever state I am, ''therewith'' to be content.
{{right|Phil. iv. 11.}}}}

2. Upon that; thereupon.

{{smaller block|"I take the privilege, Mistress Ruth, of saluting you." . . . And ''therewith'' I bussed her well.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, l.}}}}

'''therewithal''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-wiᴛ̵ʜ-âl′), ''adv.'' [Formerly also ''therewithall;'' < ''there'' + ''withal.''] 1†. With that; therewith.

{{smaller block|Knowing his voice, although not heard long sin,
She sudden was revived ''therewithall.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. xi. 44.}}}}

2†. At the same time.

{{smaller block|I bewayle mine own vnworthynesse, and ''therewithal'' do set before mine eyes the lost time of my youth mispent.
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Steele Glas (ed. Arber), Ep. Ded., p. 42.}}}}

{{smaller block|Well, give her that ring, and ''therewithal''}}

